{"Taran Pal Singh\ntaranarora1470@gmail.com | +91-7470369221 | LinkedIn | Github\nSummary\nSoftware engineer specializing in AI/ML and full-stack development. Experienced in building generative and\nagentic AI solutions with Python, LangChain, and LangGraph, backed by industry-relevant AI experience. Proficient\nin Backend (FastAPI), Frontend (React.js), and Data Structures & Algorithms. Capable of delivering intelligent,\nscalable applications across the AI and software development spectrum.\nEducation\n": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "International Institute of Information Technology, Naya Raipur\n2023 \u2013 2027\nThird year, B.Tech in Computer Science and Engineering\nGPA: 8.79/10\nScholastic Achievements\n\u2022 Secured Top 4 among 20+ teams at E-Summit Hackathon (AI/ML PS) @ IIIT Naya Raipur.\n(2026)\n\u2022 Achieved Top 15 ranking among 3,000+ participants nationwide at Hackfusion @ SPIT, Mumbai.\n(2026)\n\u2022 Presented TreadSmart: Optimizing Tyre Performance using Digital Twin as first author at ICISS.(2026)\n\u2022 Selected for Amazon ML Summer School": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "\u201925 \u2013 among Top 0.5% of 60,000+ applicants all over India.\n(2025)\n\u2022 Top 2 percentile in Joint Entrance Examination (JEE) Main among 1.1M+ candidates nationwide. (2023)\nInternship\nGenerative AI Engineer\nFebruary 2026 \u2013 April 2026\nMobineers Infosystem\n\u2022 Engineered guardrail layer over 8+ intents (Toxicity, PII, Jailbreaking, OPA etc) for custom chatbot security.\n\u2022 Compared LR, XGBoost, and Random Forest with TF-IDF for guardrail classification across 20K+ queries.\n\u2022 Fine-tuned DistilBERT and RoBER": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "Ta for guardrail classification, achieving 93% accuracy at 30ms avg latency.\n\u2022 Integrated vLLMs with NeMo as fallback, reducing inference time by 40% for real-time guardrail decisions.\n\u2022 Crafted intent classifier for 20+ intents in task management, achieving 95.2% accuracy with 0.94 F1-score.\nAI Engineering\nOctober 2025 \u2013 January 2026\nAvkalan Labs\nInternship Certificate\n\u2022 Built multimodal agentic chatbot with 92% intent accuracy over RAG and MATLAB simulations.\n\u2022 Designed simulation pipeline wit": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "h DBR-to-JSON parsing, reducing requests by 40% and setup time by 2\u00d7.\n\u2022 Implemented expert-in-the-loop escalation with priority-based routing, reducing resolution time by 35%.\nComputer Vision Intern\nAugust 2025 \u2013 November 2025\ni-Fi Healthcare Solutions\nInternship Certificate\n\u2022 Fine-tuned existing object detection models on 10 unique classes to empower over 150,000+ visually impaired\nindividuals, as part of i-Fi, a healthcare startup focused on restoring vision through technology.\n\u2022 Optimized lig": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "htweight architectures including YOLOv4-Tiny, YOLOv8n, and EfficientDet-d0, improving the\nmAP by 72.3% while maintaining real-time inference on edge devices.\nResearch Intern\nMay 2025 \u2013 July 2025\nIndian Institute of Technology, Roorkee\nInternship Certificate\n\u2022 Established back-translation pipeline with cosine similarity, increasing Punjabi training samples by 30%+.\n\u2022 Benchmarked Gemini 2.0, GPT-3.5, and Sarvam-m, achieving 60.3% improvement via advanced prompting.\nKey Projects\nAura AI - Automated": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, " Minutes & Workflows\nSeptember 2025 - October 2025\nPersonal Project\nDemo GitHub\n\u2022 Designed AI-powered meeting intelligence platform automating transcript summarization, participant identification,\nand structured action item extraction, reducing manual documentation time by 85%.\n\u2022 Developed conversational RAG-enabled task automation system integrating Composio and Trello, enabling\nnatural-language task creation and real-time progress tracking.\n\u2022 Achieved end-to-end automation with 90% faster info": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "rmation retrieval through RAG-based query processing and\nseamless tool integration.\nAutoScholar - AI Powered Research Agent\nAugust 2025 - September 2025\nGuide: Prof. Ruhul Amin | Course Project | IIIT Naya Raipur\nGitHub\n\u2022 Built an end-to-end academic research automation system using n8n and LLMs that generates IEEE-\nformatted research papers with citations, flowcharts, and APA references in under 1 minute by automating\nliterature retrieval from ArXiv, research gap analysis, and LaTeX PDF export.": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "\n\u2022 Achieved 95% time savings and 94%+ originality (validated via Turnitin), reducing manual research workflows\nby 10+ hours weekly and significantly accelerating project timelines.\nTechnical Skills\nLanguages & Core Programming: Python, C/C++, Javascript, HTML, CSS, Tailwind CSS.\nStack: FastAPI, LangChain, LangGraph, LlamaIndex LoRA, QLoRA, OpenCV, vLLM, VLMs, CrewAI, MCPs, NeMo\nGuardrails, Guardrails AI, Red Teaming, Colang, Presidio, Huggingface, Pydantic AI, Streamlit, React.js, Node.js,\nExpre": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ss.js.\nDatabases & Data Tools: MongoDB, PostgreSQL, ChromaDB, Weaviate, AstraDB, FAISS, GraphDB.\nData Structures & Algorithms: Solved 500+ problems on platforms like Leetcode, Codeforces and GeeksforGeeks\nPosition of Responsibility\nHead Placement Coordinator \u2014 Training & Placement Cell, IIIT NR\nJanuary 2025 - Present\nLed end-to-end placement operations for 200+ students, coordinating with recruiters and faculty, and conducted\ntargeted placement-preparation workshops, achieving a 90% placement ra": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "te across top-tier firms.\n": {"file": "taran_resume_format (26).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "TreadSmart: Optimizing Tyre Performance using \nDigital Twin \n \n*Taran Pal Singh\n1, Ashutosh Kumar \n1, Tarang Varshney\n1, and Ruhul Amin\n2 \n \n1IIIT Naya Raipur, Naya Raipur, India \n1taran23100@iiitnr.edu.in \n1ashutosh23101@iiitnr.edu.in \n1tarang23100@iiitnr.edu.in \n \n2IIIT Naya Raipur, Naya Raipur, India  \n2ruhul@iiitnr.edu.in \nAbstract-Simulating \ntyre \nperformance \nunder \nvarying \nenvironmental \nconditions, including extreme scenarios, is challenging yet essential for \noptimization. This resear": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ch develops a digital twin of a tyre by leveraging real-\ntime data from sensors like piezoelectric force sensor, Hall Effect Sensor, \ntemperature, wear-and-tear, and ultrasonic sensors. Using MQTT protocol and \ncloud-based IoT platforms, the data is transmitted for integration with a CAD \nmodel to create the digital twin, enabling comprehensive performance analysis, \npredictive maintenance, and fuel-efficient optimizations. The resultant Digital \nTwin system effectively models dynamic tyre behav": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "iour and confirms its utility \nin predicting wear patterns and optimizing operational parameters. This \napproach offers a scalable, cost-effective, and precise solution for enhancing \ntyre performance in real-world conditions. \nKeywords: Digital twin, mqtt, iot, predictive maintenance. \n1.  Introduction  \n \nTyres are among the most critical components of a vehicle, directly influencing \nsafety, stability, and overall performance. As the sole point of contact between the \nvehicle and the road, ty": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "res play a pivotal role in determining traction, braking \nefficiency, fuel economy, comfort, and handling. Their performance and durability \nare not only fundamental to vehicle safety but also significantly impact operational \ncosts and environmental sustainability. \nIn modern automotive applications, vehicles are subjected to diverse operating \nconditions, ranging from routine road usage to extreme environmental scenarios, \nwhile the durability of road infrastructure often falls short, with lif": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "espans shorter \nthan projected  \n [1] .Moreover, Tire performance depends upon the tyre drag force, tyre friction, \netc. [2] which adds further challenges to maintaining tyre resilience and \noptimizing performance, making tyre evaluation and improvement a priority for \nthe automotive industry.Traditional tyre testing methods, however, are constrained \nby limitations in time, cost, and the ability to replicate certain conditions, \nemphasizing the need for innovative and efficient testing approach": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "es. \n \n \n \nThe solution to these challenges lies in Digital Twin (DT) technology, which has \nemerged as a transformative tool in modern engineering and manufacturing. A DT \nis a detailed virtual representation of a \nphysical object or system, continuously \nupdated with real-time data to reflect the \ncurrent state of its physical counterpart. It \nreplicates the behavior and lifecycle of the \nphysical object, providing an accurate, \ndynamic model that evolves alongside the \nreal-world entity. The ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "concept of Digital \nTwins was first introduced by Grieves in a \n2003 presentation and later documented in a \nwhite paper, laying the foundation for its \ndevelopment [3-4]. NASA further solidified \nthe importance of Digital Twins in [5] \nmarking a key milestone in defining the \nconcept. The traditional approach to testing \nand evaluation can be costly and time-\nconsuming, especially when it involves \nreplicating extreme or challenging scenarios \nthat may only occur under specific \nconditions. Thi": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "s is why DTs are essential. \n \n \nA DT connects a physical entity and its \nvirtual representation by continuously \nexchanging \nreal-time \ndata \nthrough \nestablished connections. Sensors on the \nphysical entity gather operational data, \nwhich is transmitted to a centralized \ndatabase. The virtual entity processes this \ndata to simulate, monitor, and replicate the \nbehavior \nand \nstate \nof \nthe \nphysical \ncounterpart [6] Insights from the virtual model, such as \nperformance metrics or predictions o": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "f potential \nfailures, are sent back to optimize the physical entity. Services analyze the data to \n Fig.1 Overview of a DT \nFig.2 Usecase of DT \nsupport decision-making, enabling predictive maintenance, improved design, and \nenhanced operational efficiency. This ongoing cycle of data exchange creates a \ndynamic system that evolves alongside the physical entity, ensuring better \nperformance and reliability. \n \nDTs are widely adopted across various industries, from manufacturing to \nhealthcare, d": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ue to their ability to provide a deeper understanding of systems and \ntheir interactions with their environments. These virtual replicas enable real-time \nanalysis that helps identify potential failures, optimize designs, and enhance overall \nsystem efficiency. The integration of DTs with Internet of Things (IoT) sensors and \nArtificial Intelligence (AI) enables a more connected and intelligent approach to \nengineering and maintenance, leading to improved operational performance and \nreduced ris": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ks. \n \nOur research focuses on how digital twins are transforming the way engineers \nanalyze and develop tires in the automotive industry. By creating virtual copies of \ntires through integration of detailed CAD (Computer-Aided Design) models, \nengineers combine information from multiple sensors - pressure sensors, \ntemperature sensors, acceleration sensors, strain gauges, load sensors, vibration \nsensors, tread depth sensors, and road surface sensors. This combination of CAD \nmodels and sensor ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "data creates a comprehensive virtual setup that lets them see \nexactly how tires behave in all sorts of situations. It allows for detailed analysis for \nwear patterns, performance characteristics, and potential failure points without \nextensive physical trials.By integrating real-time \nsensor data, it also supports predictive maintenance, identifying potential failures \nbefore they occur, improving safety, and extending tyre life. \nThis approach enhances tyre efficiency, durability, and suitabil": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ity for real-world \nconditions, while also reducing downtime and repair costs, \nultimately contributing to safer, more reliable tyres and advancing automotive \nengineering. \n2.  Literature Review \nThe idea of a digital twin (DT) of a tire has been in great demand over the past few \nyears, as it allows for real-time analysis of performance and maintenance. Kaliske \net al. [1] introduced a vision of a DT framework for the road-tire-vehicle system, \nfocusing on its prospective role in mobility. [1]": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, " 's contribution emphasized the \nimportance of sensor information in optimizing vehicle dynamics and fuel \nefficiency. In a similar context, Karkaria et al. [7] introduced a machine learning-\nbased DT for tire health monitoring that promised predictive maintenance through \nthe creation of data-driven, strong models. Following this, Calabrese et al. [8] \nintroduced tire virtualization as a contemporary solution, stressing its potential in \nmimicking the behavior of the tire under various operatin": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "g conditions, thus allowing \nfor optimization and safety improvements. \n \n \nA wider view of DT technology in manufacturing came from Tao et al. [9] who \noutlined computational modeling and data fusion methods required in real-time \nsimulation. Grieves [3], in his classic book on DTs, directed attention to the \napplication of DTs in manufacturing excellence that could be directly carried over \nto the automobile sector. These studies altogether made it possible to design a DT \nfor tires that incor": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "porated sensor data from piezoelectric force, Hall Effect, \ntemperature, wear-and-tear, and ultrasonic sensors. The MQTT protocol and cloud-\nbased IoT platforms could easily broadcast and synchronize real-time sensor values \nto a CAD model in order to augment performance analysis and maximize fuel \nefficiency. Building on these advancements, our research aims to refine DT \napplications by simulating tire behavior across diverse environmental conditions, \nincluding extreme scenarios. This approac": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "h offers a cost-effective and scalable \nsolution for optimizing tire performance in real-world applications. \n \n                                   Table 1. RESEARCH PAPER ANALYSIS.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nRef    \nFocus Area \nKey Contribution  \n \nTechiques \n \nComparison with Our Work \n \n[1] \n DT for road-tire-vehicle \nsystem \nOptimizing vehicle \ndynamics and fuel \nefficiency using sensor data \nSensor-based \nDT framework \nIntegration of real-time IoT for \nenhanced maintenance \n[2] \nML-based DT for tir": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "e \nhealth \nPredictive maintenance \nthrough data-driven models \nMachine \nlearning, health \nmonitoring \nMulti-sensor integration with \nMQTT-based monitoring \n[3] \nTire virtualization \nSimulating tire behavior \nunder various conditions \nVirtual models, \nsafety \noptimization \nIncorporation of extreme \nconditions and CAD \nsimulations \n[4] \nDT in Industry \nComputational modeling \nand \ndata fusion \nData fusion, \nreal- \ntime simulation \nApplication to tires with IoT- \nbased data collection \n[5] \nDT in m": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "anufacturing \nDT as a tool for \nmanufacturing excellence \nDigital twin \nconcept \nImplementation in automotive \nsector for tire monitoring \n \n \n \n3.  Motivation & Contributions \n \n The growing need for tyres that can perform efficiently across diverse terrains and \nextreme conditions underscores the shortcomings of traditional testing methods, \nwhich often struggle to accurately replicate harsh environments. This makes it \nchallenging to ensure tyres can maintain their performance, safety, and du": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "rability \nunder such conditions. Addressing these issues is crucial for enhancing vehicle \nsafety, improving fuel efficiency, and extending tyre lifespan, which benefits \nconsumers through a safer driving experience and the automotive industry through \nreduced maintenance costs and higher customer satisfaction. \n \nOur model makes several significant contributions to the field of digital twin \ntechnology in tyre manufacturing and monitoring: \n \n\uf0b7 \nReal-time IoT Sensor Data Collection, Storage, an": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "d Preprocessing for \nTyre Monitoring: Real-time data from tyre-mounted sensors (pressure, \ntemperature, and wear) was captured via ESP32 microcontrollers and \nstored in ThingSpeak. The collected dataset was then preprocessed to \nremove inconsistencies and ensure data \nquality for subsequent analysis. \n     \n\uf0b7 \nIntegrating \nCAD Models: \nCreating \nprecise CAD models that replicate the \nphysical tyre specifications, enabling \naccurate simulation and evaluation of tyre \nperformance under extreme con": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ditions. \n \n\uf0b7 \nDevelopment of a Dynamic Digital Twin \nUsing ThingsBoard:  Using ThingsBoard, \nwe created a digital twin that integrates \nCAD models with real-time data for \ndynamic tyre performance monitoring. \n \n\uf0b7 \nSecurity Enhancement Using Blockchain: We address \nsecurity vulnerabilities in the digital twin framework by proposing \nblockchain technology to ensure data integrity, secure communication, and \nprevent tampering \n \n \n \n \nFig.3   Architecture of the model \n4 . Proposed Methodology \n ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "\nThis section aims to develop a comprehensive digital twin model for tyres that \naccurately simulates their real-time behavior under varying environmental \nconditions. This section outlines the step-by-step process adopted to integrate real-\ntime sensor data, create a high-fidelity virtual model, and simulate various \noperational scenarios to analyze tyre performance. \n \n4.1 Data Acquisition & Preprocessing \n \nSensors, including a temperature sensor, piezoelectric force sensor, \nHallEffect senso": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "r, and wear-and-tear sensor, were deployed on the tyre to \ncapture real-time operational data. This data is transmitted to the \nThingSpeak platform, where Application Programming Interface (API) \nrequests enable efficient and automated retrieval. By eliminating the need \nfor manual downloads, this approach ensures real-time access to telemetry \ndata, which is subsequently loaded into a pandas DataFrame for structured \ndata manipulation. \n \nBefore analysis, the data undergoes \npre-processing to e": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "nsure accuracy and \nconsistency. \nKey \nsteps \ninclude \nhandling missing values through \nimputation, filtering outliers using \nstatistical methods, and normalizing \nnumerical features such as force, \nrotational speed, temperature, and \nwear-and-tear. \nThe \npre-processed \ndata is iterated for detailed analysis \nand modeling. This streamlined \nprocess forms the foundation of the \ndigital twin model, providing accurate \nsimulations and insights into tyre \n \n4.2 CAD Model Creation \n \nThe creation of ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "a detailed 3D CAD \nmodel of a tire involves using \nadvanced CAD tools like Blender, \nCreo, or SolidWorks to replicate the \ntire\u2019s \nphysical \nstructure \nand \ndimensions \n[10]. \nThis \nmodel \nincorporates essential features such as \ntread patterns, material properties, \nand wear characteristics, ensuring it accurately represents the real-world \nbehavior of the tire. The model is crafted with precise dimensional \nFig.4  Layer 1 of the model \naccuracy, capturing the tire\u2019s radius, width, sidewall des": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ign, and internal \nstructure, such as the bead area and casing. Material properties like \nelasticity, durability, and heat resistance are embedded, allowing for \nsimulations that reflect the tire's performance in various driving conditions \nand environments. \n \n \n \n \n                             Fig. 5 Different Views of Cad Model \n \n4.3 Database Integration \n \nFollowing the creation of the CAD model, real-time sensor data is \nintegrated with a cloud-based IoT platform, such as ThingsBoard, usin": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "g \nMessage Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) Communication and Data \nSerialization. This process ensures seamless data flow and compatibility \nwith the digital twin framework. \n \nThe MQTT protocol is implemented using the paho.mqtt.client library to \nfacilitate the transmission of telemetry data. The key steps include: \n \n\uf0b7 \nClient Initialization: The MQTT client is configured with an access \ntoken to securely authenticate the device with the ThingsBoard \nplatform. \n\uf0b7 \nConnection Management: A ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "robust connection monitoring \nmechanism is implemented via on_connect callback function to log \nconnection status and ensure reliable communication. \n\uf0b7 \nData Publishing: Telemetry data is transmitted to the designated \ntopic  v1/devices/me/telemetry, adhering to ThingsBoard\u2019s MQTT \nschema for structured data handling. \n \nTelemetry data is serialized into JSON format using Python\u2019s json library \nto ensure compatibility with ThingsBoard\u2019s REST-like data ingestion \ninterface. Each dataset row is dy": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "namically transformed into key-value \npairs, \nwith \nthe \nkeys \nrepresenting \nsensor \nattributes \n(e.g., \n\"air_temperature\", \"rotational_speed\"). This structured format enables \nseamless ingestion and processing of real-time sensor data within the \ndigital twin ecosystem \n \n \nAlgorithm 1   Telementry Data Transimssion To ThingsBoard \n \n \nRequire : file_path:  to Excel file with telemetry data. \nEnsure: Telemetry data sent to ThingsBoard device \n     1: Load dataset: data \u2190 read excel(f ile_path) ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "\n     2: Initialize MQTT client with ThingsBoard credentials \n     3: Connect to ThingsBoard Cloud \n     4: Function send_telemetry(row): \n     5:     Create telemetry JSON with sensor readings and failure \n         flags \n     6:      Publish: client.publish(\u2019v1/devices/me/telemetry\u2019, data) \n     7: for each row row in data do \n     8:      send telemetry(row) \n     9:      Wait 1 second \n     10: end for \n     11: Disconnect client \n \n \n4.4 Dynamic Digital Twin using Thingsboard \n \nThe dynamic": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, " digital twin implementation integrates the CAD model with \nreal-time sensor data through ThingsBoard's widget system [11], \nemploying a three-layer architecture: visualization, data binding, and \ndynamic response. The visualization layer renders the CAD model within \nThingsBoard's custom widget framework, while the data binding layer \nmaps incoming sensor telemetry to specific model parameters. The \ndynamic response layer handles real-time updates and state transitions, \nmaintaining synchroniza": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "tion between physical sensor readings and the \nvirtual model's state at 1Hz refresh rate. \n \nThe system incorporates real-time analysis capabilities through \nThingsBoard's Rule Engine, featuring continuous monitoring of \noperational parameters and automatic anomaly detection. The interactive \ninterface provides manual rotation and zoom controls for detailed \ninspection, along with temperature and stress distribution heat maps. The \ndigital twin employs WebGL acceleration for rendering and implem": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ents \nadaptive refresh rates based on parameter change frequency, ensuring \noptimal performance while maintaining visual fidelity. This enables users \nto monitor tire conditions, predict maintenance requirements, and conduct \nwhat-if scenarios through parameter adjustment sliders, all within a \ncohesive digital environment \n \n5  Experimental Results \n \nThe proposed model leverages ThingsBoard, an IoT device management and \nvisualization platform, to deploy and monitor digital twins effectively. ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "Using this \nmodel, real-time data from a tire's digital twin was integrated, analyzed, and \nvisualized to demonstrate its scalability for various use cases. Key observations \nfrom the experiment include: \n \nTemperature Monitoring:   The model captured real-time air and internal \ntemperatures of the tire. The air temperature averaged 298 K, while the internal \ntemperature was 309 K, providing insights into the tire\u2019s thermal stability. This \nfeature can be extended to monitor temperature conditio": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ns for any industrial \nequipment or process. \n \n                            Fig. 6. Temperature Analysis \n \nTool Wear Analysis:   In Fig 6 a line chart highlighted with an average wear time \nof 127.3 minutes. This capability allows predictive maintenance for machinery by \nidentifying wear trends, making the model adaptable for monitoring components \nacross industries. \n \n \n \nPerformance Monitoring and 3D Visualization:   The proposed advanced \nmonitoring model demonstrates sophisticated capabili": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "ties in tracking and \nanalyzing critical mechanical performance metrics through an integrated approach \ncombining high-precision data acquisition and immersive 3D visualization \ntechnologies. By leveraging sophisticated sensor instrumentation and \ncomputational modeling, the system provides comprehensive insights into \ncomplex mechanical dynamics. \n \n \n \n                        Fig. 8. Rotational Speed Analysis \nFig.7 Tool Wear Analysis \n \n                                          Fig. 9. Torque": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, " Analysis \n \nRotational speed measurements revealed a consistent average of 1526.2 kHz, \nindicating stable operational characteristics across simulated conditions. The torque \nanalysis exposed nuanced variations with an average of 41.615 Nm, highlighting \nthe model's sensitivity in capturing subtle mechanical interactions. These precise \nmeasurements underscore the system's capacity to detect and quantify intricate \nperformance parameters in real-time mechanical environments. \nThe innovative 3D ": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "rendering component introduces a transformative visualization \nmethodology, enabling spatial analysis of mechanical components\u2014in this \ninstance, a comprehensive tire behavior simulation. This advanced visualization \ntechnique transcends traditional monitoring approaches by providing multi-\ndimensional perspectives on mechanical system interactions, facilitating deeper \nunderstanding of complex structural and operational dynamics. \n \n6. Conclusion \n \nDigital twin technology transforms tire perfo": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "rmance analysis through an innovative \nintegration of IoT sensors, advanced CAD modeling, and real-time data processing. \nThe TreadSmart system captures critical mechanical parameters including \ntemperature, rotational speed, and torque, enabling unprecedented insights into tire \ndynamics. By implementing cutting-edge security protocols, the research provides \nengineers a powerful tool for predictive maintenance and comprehensive \nperformance monitoring across diverse operational scenarios. \n \nF": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "uture research directions include leveraging blockchain technology to enhance \ndata integrity and security. Blockchain's decentralized and immutable ledger can \nprovide additional layers of protection for sensor data, preventing unauthorized \nmodifications and ensuring transparent, tamper-proof record-keeping. This \napproach would further strengthen the digital twin's reliability by creating an \nauditable trail of tire performance metrics and maintenance records. \n \nThe proposed digital twin fra": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "mework represents a transformative advancement in \nautomotive engineering, transcending traditional performance monitoring \nmethodologies. By synthesizing real-time sensor data, advanced computational \nmodeling, and immersive 3D visualization, this innovative approach provides \nengineers and researchers with unprecedented insights into complex mechanical \ninteractions. Its adaptable architecture ensures scalability across multiple industrial \ndomains, offering a comprehensive solution for unders": {"file": "digital_twin[1] (3).pdf", "type": "pdf"}, "tanding, predicting, and \noptimizing mechanical system behaviors with remarkable precision and depth. \n \n            